= Generalization: Simple =
<onlyinclude>'''Generalization''' means using what we have learned from some examples or situations to make a claim or act in others. It is useful, but it can fail when the new situation differs in an important way.</onlyinclude>

== A familiar example ==
Suppose several plants of one species grow well in a sunny garden. It is reasonable to investigate whether other plants of that species will do well in similar sunlight. It is not yet justified to say that ''every'' plant of the species will thrive in ''every'' climate. Soil, water, temperature, and selection of the original plants may matter.

== Three ways the word is used ==
* '''Reasoning:''' drawing a broader, uncertain conclusion from observed cases.
* '''Learning:''' using an idea or skill in a new setting, such as applying a mathematical principle to a new kind of problem.
* '''Research:''' deciding whether a study's findings apply to people or conditions not directly studied.

== How to judge a generalization ==
Ask: What was actually observed? What is the new situation? What could differ? What evidence would reveal a mistake? A useful generalization is explicit about its limits, not merely confident.

== What it does not mean ==
Generalization is not the same as a logically certain proof. Nor does success on familiar examples guarantee success in a different context. For the specific use in predictive models, see [[Generalization (machine learning)]].

